# Every path is owned by @vijay-tylertech, so every pull request needs his approval while
# this review workflow is still bedding in. Combined with branch protection's
# "Require review from Code Owners", that is what makes the approval mandatory rather
# than advisory.
#
# Note the deliberate asymmetry: because a PR author can never approve their own PR,
# @vijay-tylertech would be permanently blocked on his own changes if administrators were
# also forced through the gate. Branch protection therefore leaves "Do not allow bypassing"
# OFF, so repo admins can merge their own work while everyone else waits for review.
# The practical effect, given branch protection exempts admins:
#   @tyler-technologies/onetyler-tcp-pm-admins       admin  -> can merge without review
#   @tyler-technologies/onetyler-tcp-pm-contributors write  -> PRs need approval below
# So put reviewers on the CONTRIBUTORS team. Putting someone on the admins team removes the
# review requirement from them, which is the opposite of the intent.
#
# Loosen this to a team (e.g. @tyler-technologies/onetyler-tcp-pm-admins) once the team is
# comfortable with the process.

*                       @vijay-tylertech

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE INSTRUCTION SET — admin-only by policy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below tells a human or an AI agent HOW to work in this repo, or gates how
# contributions are checked. A contributor changing any of it changes the rules under which
# their own work is judged, and an AI agent that edits its own instructions mid-session then
# proceeds to follow the edited version. That is the specific failure this section names.
#
# The ONLY path a contributor is expected to write to is /transcripts/ — their review
# verdicts. The review UI enforces this from the other end: its Git & PR tab stages
# `transcripts/` and nothing else.
#
# These entries are not stricter than the `*` rule above — one owner cannot be narrowed
# further. They are here so the boundary is explicit and survives `*` being loosened to a
# team later: whoever does that must decide deliberately whether to loosen these too.
/CLAUDE.md                      @vijay-tylertech
/README.md                      @vijay-tylertech
/contributor-initial-prompt.md  @vijay-tylertech
/transcripts/README.md          @vijay-tylertech
/transcripts/ONBOARDING.md      @vijay-tylertech
/scripts/                       @vijay-tylertech
/templates/                     @vijay-tylertech
/.github/                       @vijay-tylertech
/.gitignore                     @vijay-tylertech
